<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Transportation – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Transportation</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Benefits of Public Transportation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Facilitate a discussion on a group project. Concept: Interpret graphical information for classmates.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can facilitate a group discussion on transportation options by encouraging participation and managing turn-taking, using supportive gestures and prompts to guide the flow of ideas (e.g., gestures toward the speaker to indicate their turn and asks, "Who would like to share their thoughts on the best route?"). Can interpret a transportation map by pointing out key locations and using simple explanations (e.g., points to the bus stop in Boquete and explain that it leads to the David terminal, where travelers can catch a bus to Panama City or other destinations). Can explain public transportation rules using simple words and two languages when necessary (e.g., describes "fare" in both English and familiar language).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Teacher displays an image of a crowded street with public transportation options (bus, taxi, sidewalk for walking). Students discuss in pairs what they see, which methods they prefer, and why (e.g., "The bus is more convenient than a car because you avoid traffic."). Teacher asks students to name some benefits of public transportation. Teacher writes their ideas on the board.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> Teacher models how to suggest, compare, and explain. "Today, we'll learn how to mediate—this means helping others understand information, like when you explain a map or rules. For example, I might say, 'This route is the fastest because it avoids rush hour traffic.' or 'You must tap in when you get on the bus.' I will also model the stress in words like 'transportation' and 'passenger' and distinguish between /æ/ in 'tap' and /eɪ/ in 'tape'."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does it mean to 'mediate' in a discussion?" (Expected answer: To help people understand or discuss something.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'The bus is cheaper than a taxi,' am I comparing or obligating?" (Expected answer: Comparing.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Transportation".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Teacher presents a simple, color-coded transportation map of a fictional or simplified city area (e.g., "Panama City Downtown routes") along with a short list of public transportation rules/protocols. The map will feature bus stops, train stations, and key landmarks, using words like 'route', 'destination', 'fare', 'schedule', 'traffic light', 'highway'. The rules will include phrases like "You should wear your seatbelt," "You have to pay the fare," and "Please avoid talking loudly." Some rules will be presented in simple English, with a familiar language translation provided for "fare" or "tap in" to model the learning outcome (e.g., "Fare (tarifa) must be paid upon boarding.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will interpret visual information (map) and written instructions (rules) to explain them to others.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify the fastest and cheapest routes on the map using comparatives and superlatives.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify two essential rules for passengers and their importance.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students match phrases from the rules to their meaning (e.g., "tap in" to "register your journey").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using modal verbs for advice and obligation ("should," "have to"), comparatives/superlatives ("faster," "safest," "more convenient"), and phrasal verbs ("get on," "get off," "tap in") to interpret and explain transportation information.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Mediation Phrase Matching:</b> Teacher provides a worksheet with common mediation phrases (e.g., "Let me explain this part," "What do you think about this option?", "To clarify, this means...") and students match them to their function (e.g., "Explaining," "Encouraging participation," "Confirming understanding"). They also complete sentences with target vocabulary and grammar (e.g., "The bus is _____ (fast) than the car during rush hour." or "You _____ (obligation) wear a seatbelt."). Teacher provides feedback on accuracy.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Explaining Routes & Rules:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a different simplified transportation route or rule from the presented material. One student describes the route or rule to their partner, using modals, comparatives, and phrasal verbs, while the partner listens and asks for clarification using mediation phrases. For instance, "This route is the most efficient because it avoids the main highway. You should get on at stop A." The partner might ask, "So, to clarify, we avoid the main highway? What about the fare?" The teacher circulates, correcting pronunciation of multisyllabic words like 'efficient' and 'schedule', and addressing vowel contrasts in minimal pairs like 'passenger' and 'port'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Project Map Preview:</b> In preparation for the 21st Century Skills project, students form their project groups. Each student takes turns briefly explaining one feature or rule from their *own* developing transportation map design to their group members, using the target mediation language and encouraging feedback. (e.g., "I designed this new bus route. I think it's the safest way to travel because it has dedicated bus lanes. What do you guys think?"). This activates their project-specific mediation needs.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Transportation".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Project Presentation & Discussion. Students, in their project groups, present their "Transportation Map Design" to the class (or to another group). Each student within the group *must* take a turn mediating part of the presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group displays their designed transportation map.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take turns facilitating the discussion and interpreting different sections of their map and its associated rules/benefits. For example:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student 1 (Facilitator): "Who would like to share their thoughts on the best route for tourists? Mario, could you explain the express bus route?" (Using gestures to indicate turn-taking and encouraging participation).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student 2 (Interpreter): "Okay, I'll explain. This express bus (points to map) is the fastest option for tourists because it avoids local stops. Travelers should get on at the airport. The fare is $2.50. (points to a key) To clarify, 'fare' here is 'tarifa'." (Interpreting graphical information and explaining rules using two languages where necessary).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student 3 (Explainer/Comparator): "This route is more convenient than the private car because it offers a direct connection to the city center and you can avoid traffic. You have to buy your ticket in advance for this route. It is the most efficient way to travel." (Making suggestions for convenience, emphasizing safety through rules).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student 4 (Sociolinguistic Role-play/Protocol): "Remember, following local protocols, drivers must remind passengers to wear seatbelts, especially for longer journeys. So, if you hop on a taxi, expect the driver to say, 'Please put your seatbelt on for your safety!'"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher observes and takes notes on students' use of mediation strategies, target grammar, vocabulary, and pronunciation.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Teacher uses an observation checklist/rubric during the group project presentations (Stage 4). Students will be assessed on their ability to facilitate discussions, interpret their map, and explain transportation rules/benefits using the target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Rubric focuses on):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mediation Skills:</b> Effectively facilitates discussion (encourages participation, manages turn-taking), clearly interprets graphical information (map), explains rules/concepts clearly (using two languages if necessary).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Language Use (A2.4):</b> Consistent use of modal verbs (should, have to), comparatives/superlatives (faster, safest), and target vocabulary (route, fare, convenience, traffic, passenger).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Intelligible pronunciation, with an effort to stress multisyllabic words ('transportation', 'convenience') and distinguish short/long vowels ('tap'/'tape', 'hop'/'hope').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Engagement & Collaboration:</b> Actively participates in group presentation, responds appropriately to questions, and works collaboratively.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new English phrase I used today to help someone understand was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it easy/challenging to (explain a rule / facilitate a discussion / interpret the map) because..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of explaining or discussing our transportation map did I do best today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one specific thing I can improve when helping my classmates understand information?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What worked well in this mediation-focused lesson? (e.g., student engagement with the map project, use of specific mediation phrases). Many students successfully used comparatives to describe routes, like 'This route is faster.' I heard good examples of mediating language for explaining rules like 'To clarify...'"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students to further develop their mediation skills, especially for interpreting complex information or translating when necessary? Perhaps more targeted practice on explaining complex rules or using familiar language strategically. Some struggled with turn-taking and encouraging others; clearer sentence stems for facilitation might help."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How does this lesson connect to the next lesson in the theme sequence? (This is the final lesson of the sequence. Future lessons on other themes will build on these mediation skills, applying them to new contexts, potentially with more complex texts or diagrams at an A2.4+ level.)"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>